    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Let's get back to the task at hand.\n\nWhen it comes to isolating the affected container, it's essential to prevent the temperature rise from spreading to other containers. This can be achieved by using isolation valves or by diverting the refrigeration flow to other containers. It's like a group of goblins trying to escape a trap - you need to cut off their escape routes to prevent them from causing chaos.\n\nRegarding sensor reliability, if the refrigeration unit failed unexpectedly, it's crucial to verify the accuracy of the temperature sensors. You can do this by checking the sensor's calibration history, consulting the manufacturer's documentation, or by using a backup sensor to confirm the readings. It's also a good idea to review the sensor's maintenance history to ensure it was properly maintained and calibrated before the failure.\n\nIn the case of a refrigeration unit failure, it's also essential to consider the possibility of a \"goblin in the system\" - a malfunction or a hidden issue that's causing the failure. You need to investigate the root cause of the failure to prevent similar incidents in the future.\n\nNow, let's focus on the protocol for batch isolation and verification. You",
